#import "/shared/book.typ": *
#let p = problem.with(code: "MM8036", unit: "U4")
#let items = (
p(1,"E","U4.2 Curry–Howard",[Write a proof term for $A->B->A$.],[Introduce both assumptions.],[Return the first assumption.],[$lambda a.lambda b.a$ has the required type: under $a:A,b:B$ the variable $a$ has type $A$, and two abstractions discharge $b$ then $a$.],[Explain assumption discharge.]),
p(2,"E","U4.2 Conjunction",[Construct $(A times B)->A$ and $(A times B)->B$.],[Use product elimination.],[Use the two projections.],[The functions are $lambda p.pi_1(p)$ and $lambda p.pi_2(p)$. On an input pair $(a,b)$ they compute to $a$ and $b$, respectively.],[Give their computation on a constructor.]),
p(3,"E","U4.2 Disjunction",[Construct $A->A+B$ and explain what data the output contains.],[Choose the left constructor.],[Keep the supplied evidence for $A$.],[Send $a$ to the left injection of $a$. The output includes both the tag “left” and the evidence $a:A$; no evidence for $B$ is required.],[Do not replace a constructive sum by an untagged assertion.]),
p(4,"E","U4.1 Normalization vocabulary",[State weak and strong normalization and their implication relation.],[One asks for a successful path; the other quantifies over all paths.],[Every infinite path must be excluded for strong normalization.],[Weak normalization means existence of a finite reduction to a normal form. Strong normalization means absence of every infinite reduction sequence. In the finitely branching term system, strong normalization implies weak normalization by repeatedly choosing a step until none is possible. The converse fails by the ignored-$Omega$ example.],[Give the direction and a counterexample to its converse.]),
p(5,"C","U4.2 Negation",[Construct $(A->B)->((B->0)->(A->0))$.],[Assume evidence for $A$ and aim at the empty type.],[Compose the two functions.],[The term $lambda f.lambda n.lambda a.n(f a)$ works. Under $f:A->B,n:B->0,a:A$, application first gives $f a:B$ and then $n(f a):0$. Discharging the three assumptions yields the stated type.],[This is constructive contraposition; no converse is asserted.]),
p(6,"C","U4.2 Sum elimination",[Construct $((A->C) times(B->C))->((A+B)->C)$ and state its two computation cases.],[Project the two functions and inspect the sum.],[Apply the function matching the tag.],[Given pair $(f,g)$, send a left input $a$ to $f a$ and a right input $b$ to $g b$. These clauses are the sum eliminator with constant result type $C$, and its beta rules give exactly those outputs.],[Both branches must produce the same target type.]),
p(7,"C","U4.1 Candidate closure",[Explain why every variable belongs to every reducibility candidate defined in Lecture 7.],[Use neutral closure, not a typing assignment.],[A variable has no immediate reducts.],[Variables are neutral. The condition that every immediate reduct be reducible holds vacuously because there are no such reducts. Candidate property (3) therefore puts any variable in $cal(R)_A$ for every simple type $A$. This is a property of raw terms; it does not assign all types to a variable in a fixed typing context.],[Distinguish reducibility membership from a typing judgment.]),
p(8,"C","U4.1 Why strengthen induction",[Show that strong normalization of $s$ and $t$ does not imply strong normalization of $s t$.],[Use a normal abstraction that duplicates its argument.],[Take both terms to be $Delta=lambda x.x x$.],[Set $s=t=Delta=lambda x.x x$. The term $Delta$ has no beta redex, so each copy is strongly normalizing. Their application is $Omega$, which reduces to itself forever. Thus an application proof needs the arrow candidate condition controlling all reducible arguments, rather than only the termination of the function and argument separately.],[Check normality of $Delta$ and divergence of its application.]),
p(9,"C","U4.2 Double negation",[Construct $A->((A->0)->0)$ and explain why reversing the arrow requires a different argument.],[An assumption of $A$ can be fed to its negation.],[Return $lambda n.n a$.],[The full term is $lambda a.lambda n.n a$. It constructs double-negation evidence from $a:A$. For the reverse direction, an input of type $(A->0)->0$ expects a refuter of $A$; it does not directly supply a term of $A$. The construction just given cannot be run backwards, and the general reverse principle is not adopted as an axiom of the constructive core.],[Give the term without claiming that failure to find a reverse term alone proves uninhabitability.]),
p(10,"A","U4.1 Canonicity consequence",[Using normalization, preservation, and #link(label("MM8036-U3.12"))[U3.12], prove every closed term of type $o$ reduces to $c$ in the arrow-only calculus with sole constant $c:o$.],[Each theorem supplies one step in the argument.],[Normalize first, preserve the type, then classify the normal form.],[Strong normalization gives a finite reduction to some normal $n$. Repeated preservation gives $tack.r n:o$, and reduction preserves closedness by the substitution free-variable bound. #link(label("MM8036-U3.12"))[U3.12] classifies such a closed normal term as $c$. Hence the original term reduces to $c$. Confluence additionally shows that any normal form reached is the same, but is not needed to establish existence of this reduction.],[Make the closedness and type of the normal form explicit.]),
p(11,"A","U4.2 Constructive equivalence",[Construct maps in both directions between $(A+B)->C$ and $(A->C) times(B->C)$. Show the composites agree pointwise on constructor inputs.],[Restrict a function along each injection; reverse by cases.],[Test the resulting functions at left and right inputs.],[Map $h$ to the pair sending $a$ to $h("inl"(a))$ and $b$ to $h("inr"(b))$. Reverse a pair $(f,g)$ by sum elimination. Starting with $h$, the composite applied to a left input computes to $h("inl"(a))$, and similarly for a right input. Starting with $(f,g)$, its two resulting functions compute at any $a,b$ to $f(a),g(b)$. This proves the asserted pointwise computation behavior; an identity of functions in intensional identity types would require additional reasoning and is not claimed here.],[Separate pointwise computation from an assumed function-extensionality principle.]),
p(12,"A","U4.1 Normalization proof dependencies",[Reconstruct the abstraction case of the fundamental reducibility lemma. Identify exactly where the controlled beta-expansion lemma is used.],[The arrow clause needs strong normalization of the abstraction and reducibility of every application.],[Use two environment extensions: a fresh variable and an arbitrary reducible argument.],[Given a reducible environment for the free context, rename the binder $x$ fresh and let $s$ be its substituted body. Extend the environment by mapping $x$ to a fresh variable. The body induction hypothesis gives a strongly normalizing renamed copy of $s$, hence $s$ and its abstraction are strongly normalizing. For any $u in cal(R)_A$, extend the environment instead by $x:=u$; induction yields $s[x:=u] in cal(R)_B$. Since $s,u$ are strongly normalizing, controlled beta expansion gives $(lambda x.s)u in cal(R)_B$. Both clauses of $cal(R)_(A->B)$ are now satisfied.],[Supply both environment extensions and both parts of the arrow definition.]),
)
